  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Let $0<\\alpha<1$. Suppose two points are picked randomly from the interval $[0,1]$. What is the probability that the distance between the two numbers is at least $\\alpha$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(1-\\alpha)^2$.\n\nUse the combined area of the two corner triangles in the unit square.",
      "worked": "Let the chosen numbers be $x$ and $y$. Their possible pairs fill the unit square uniformly. The condition $|x-y|\\ge\\alpha$ selects two corner triangles, bounded by $y=x+\\alpha$ and $y=x-\\alpha$. Each triangle has perpendicular sides of length $1-\\alpha$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}A_{\\text{favourable}}&=2\\times\\frac12(1-\\alpha)^2\\\\\\Pr(|x-y|\\ge\\alpha)&=\\frac{(1-\\alpha)^2}{1}\\\\\\Pr(|x-y|\\ge\\alpha)&=(1-\\alpha)^2.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 36
  },
